<title>SWEDEN: TABLE-Volvo H1 profit 7.95 bln skr.</title>
<headline>TABLE-Volvo H1 profit 7.95 bln skr.</headline>
<dateline>STOCKHOLM 1997-07-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Six months to June 30, 1997,</p>
<p>					(million crowns unless stated)</p>
<p> Profit after fin items		 7,945  vs   3,856</p>
<p> Turnover				  89,024  vs  78,059</p>
<p> Operating profit			 3,948  vs   2,281</p>
<p> Operating profit, car division     2,154  vs     187</p>
<p> Operating profit, truck division     688  vs   1,078</p>
<p> Operating costs			 67,973  vs 60,370</p>
<p> Gross profit			    21,051  vs 17,689</p>
<p> R&amp;D costs				  4,489  vs  4,255</p>
<p> Associated interests		   3,080  vs    221</p>
<p> Income from other shareholdings	334  vs    826</p>
<p> Tax					  1,405  vs  1,129</p>
<p> Net profit				 6,462  vs  2,754</p>
<p> Shr (crowns, excluding share sales)   11  vs    8.5</p>
<p>NOTE -- Swedish vehicle maker AB Volvo was expected to post a pre-tax profit of 7.80 billion crowns for the first six months of 1997 against 3.86 billion crowns a year earlier, a Reuters poll showed.</p>
<p>This would include one-off profits of 3.03 billion crowns from the sale of its Pripps Ringnes stake and of 221 million crowns from the sale of a stake in airline SAS. -- Simon Haydon, Stockholm newsroom +46-8-700-1004</p>
</text>
